"The surah relates that when the hypocrites came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), they bore witness saying, 'Truly you are the Messenger of Allah,' yet they did so not with sincerity, but merely to appear Muslim on the outside. In response to their false testimony, Allah Almighty states that He knows the Prophet is indeed His messenger, but Himself bears witness that the hypocrites are liars. A hypocrite is someone whose inward does not match their outward, who is outwardly a Muslim and inwardly a disbeliever. Those meant here are Abdullah ibn Ubayy and his supporters. The emphatic expressions in their words are in fact an effort to convince the Prophet that they believe; yet they swear oaths even though they know they are lying.\n\nThe hypocrites take their oaths as a shield. By appearing outwardly Muslim, they seek through these oaths to protect their lives and wealth in this world. In this way, they turn away from the path of Allah or strive to bar others from the true religion. How evil an immorality is this conduct of theirs!\n\nWhen you see them, their outward appearance pleases you; and when they speak, the beauty of their words affects you. Yet they are like hollow timbers propped up; they fear every loud shout, thinking it is against them. Because they are treacherous, they are in constant anxiety. May Allah curse them; how they are turned away from truth to falsehood!\n\nWhen it is said to them, 'Come, let the Messenger of Allah ask forgiveness for you,' they turn their heads away and act arrogantly. It is all the same whether you ask forgiveness for them or do not ask forgiveness for them; Allah will never forgive them. For Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people. This ayah was revealed after the Messenger of Allah said he would ask forgiveness more than seventy times, following the verse previously revealed in Surah at-Tawbah.\n\nThe hypocrites say, 'Do not spend on those who are with the Messenger of Allah so that they will disperse.' However, to Allah belong the treasures of the heavens and the earth; He is the Provider of sustenance. These words of theirs stem from their ignorance. Regarding the occasion of revelation of this ayah, the narration of Zayd ibn Arqam is significant. Zayd heard this statement from Abdullah ibn Ubayy and informed the Messenger of Allah; however, when Abdullah denied it under oath, Zayd felt grief until revelation descended.\n\nThe hypocrites also say, 'When we return to Medina, the more honorable will surely expel the more humiliated from it.' Yet honor (izzah) belongs only to Allah, His Messenger, and the believers. The hypocrites do not know this; they mistake their arrogance for honor. Whereas true honor lies in a person knowing their soul and not debasing it. As for the believers, they prostrate to none other than Allah; therefore, true honor belongs to them.\n\nO you who believe! Let not your wealth or your children distract you from the remembrance of Allah. And whoever does that, they are the losers. And spend from what We have provided you before death comes to one of you and he says, 'My Lord, if only You would delay me for a short while so that I might give charity and be among the righteous.' But never will Allah delay a soul when its appointed term has come. And Allah is fully aware of what you do.\n\nIn the ninth ayah, addressing the believers, Allah commands that neither wealth nor children should distract them from remembering Allah. Here, preoccupation with wealth and children—which are the fleeting adornments of worldly life—must not cause one to forget the remembrance of Allah, which is the primary objective. The purpose is not to have no concern for them at all, but rather that concern for them should not become so excessive as to make one forget Allah and His pleasure.\n\nThe remembrance of Allah (dhikrullah) entails acts of worship such as prayer, as well as contemplating Allah and bearing in mind His commands and prohibitions. Whoever becomes preoccupied with the pursuit of wealth and children and forgets Allah, those are the ones who are in loss. For worldly life passes away, and in the Hereafter only enduring righteous deeds are of benefit.\n\nIn the ayah, two qualities are emphasized for believers to attain honor: not being negligent of the remembrance of Allah and spending (infaq) from the sustenance He has provided. These two matters bring to mind a hadith concerning the discussion among the angels: the angels confer over the expiations (kaffarat, deeds that expiate sins) and degrees (darajat, deeds that elevate ranks).\n\nThe expiations are walking to good deeds, remaining seated in the mosque after prayer, and performing ablution. The degrees are feeding others, spreading the greeting of peace, and praying at night. The Messenger of Allah commanded us to learn these deeds as a lesson. Infaq (spending in Allah's cause) in particular is the bridge that leads a person to true honor; after obligatory dues are paid, supererogatory charities elevate ranks.\n\nTrue honor lies not in consuming food, but in feeding others. Those who spend their wealth solely on themselves and give no thought to others are at a low level. Noble societies, on the other hand, race with one another in spending in charity; the angels' discussions are also about expiations and degrees. The religion of Islam has commanded the remembrance of Allah and spending in His cause in order to raise believers to this high honor.\n\nIn the eleventh ayah, it is stated that Allah will never delay any soul when its appointed term comes, and that He is fully aware of all that you do. Therefore, before death arrives, we must not forget Allah and we must spend in His way. This ayah reminds us that gain and loss will become manifest on the Day of Reckoning; Surah at-Taghabun elaborates further on this subject."